<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/nvalt-generic-header-rb.7540.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>

<p>I took another pass at the editor to fill in what I considered to be missing pieces. The <a href="https://brettterpstra.com/projects/nvalt/">nvALT project page</a> has the full writeup. This is the short version of the things I released this morning.</p>

<h3 id="markers">Markers</h3>

<p><strong>Editing</strong> settings now has a choice for bold, italic, and unordered lists. Bold is <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">**</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__</code>. Italic is <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">*</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">_</code>. A new bullet is <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">*</code>, or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">+</code>. The defaults are still asterisks, which is what Command-B, Command-I, and Option-Command-L have been inserting.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="gs">**asterisks**</span> or __underscores__
<span class="ge">*asterisk*</span> or _underscore_</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Pick the ones you want. The shortcuts use that choice from then on. Existing notes are left alone.</p>

<h3 id="lists-more-than-one-line">Lists, more than one line</h3>

<p>Option-Command-L on one line is unchanged. Plain text becomes a bullet, a bullet becomes a number, a number goes back to plain text.</p>

<p>Select more than one line and the same cycle applies to the whole selection. Indents stay where they are. Blank lines stay blank.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>milk
eggs
    sourdough</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The first press uses whatever bullet you chose:</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">-</span> milk
<span class="p">-</span> eggs
<span class="p">    -</span> sourdough</code></pre></div></div>

<p>If every item is already a bullet, the next press numbers them. Nested lines count on their own.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">1.</span> milk
<span class="p">2.</span> eggs
<span class="p">    1.</span> sourdough</code></pre></div></div>

<p>One more press removes the markers and leaves the indent behind. If the selection is mixed, some lines marked and some not, that press clears the markers instead of numbering. The selection stays put, so you can walk the cycle again.</p>

<h3 id="backticks">Backticks</h3>

<p>Auto-pair now knows about backticks. The first one inserts a pair and leaves the cursor between them. The next one types over the closer, which is the inline-code case:</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="sb">`code`</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>A third backtick does not wrap another pair. It stays a single character, so three in a row is a fence, and more backticks lengthen that run. A backtick typed right against a letter is left alone, and a selected word gets wrapped.</p>

<p>I had already added some cool completion when you type an opening fence and hit return, where it will insert a closing fence with your cursor on a blank line between them. This also works if you add a language to the code fence.</p>

<h3 id="reference-links">Reference links</h3>

<p>Typing the id in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[text][id]</code>, or in an image link <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">![alt][id]</code>, offers the definition ids already in the note. This note already has <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">spec</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">diagram</code> defined:</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>See <span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">the spec</span><span class="p">][</span><span class="ss">spec</span><span class="p">]</span> and !<span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">diagram</span><span class="p">][</span><span class="ss">diagram</span><span class="p">]</span>.

<span class="p">[</span><span class="ss">spec</span><span class="p">]:</span> <span class="sx">https://example.com/spec</span>
<span class="p">[</span><span class="ss">diagram</span><span class="p">]:</span> <span class="sx">https://example.com/diagram.png</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>Put the cursor after <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[more][</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">spec</code> is in the list. Keep typing and the list narrows. Return, Tab, or a click accepts one. If the closing bracket is already there, it stays and the cursor moves past it.</p>

<p>A <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[</code> at the start of a line (or within three spaces of it) is a new definition. That autocomplete list is the other direction: ids the note already uses that do not have a definition yet.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">CommonMark</span><span class="p">][]</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>Start the next line with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CommonMark</code> is offered. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[[wiki links]]</code> still complete note titles, and none of this runs inside a fenced code block.</p>

<h3 id="addendum-theme-colors">Addendum: theme colors</h3>

<p>Two color bugs that showed up once a theme was on.</p>

<p>Autocomplete was drawing the suggestion in black. The letters you had already typed went black too, and so did the brackets around a link. That text now stays the theme&rsquo;s foreground color for as long as the completion is open. Accept it or dismiss it and the normal syntax colors come back.</p>

<p>The word count in the search field was a light cyan token on dark themes, Svelte in particular, and it was hard to read. That color was the system token tint, not anything in the theme file. The count is plain text now, in a color that contrasts with the white search field.</p>

<h3 id="addendum-note-links-in-the-preview">Addendum: note links in the preview</h3>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[[wiki links]]</code> already open a note from the editor. In the preview they were just text. They are links now.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>See [[Meeting notes]] for the rest.</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The brackets stay visible. Clicking one opens the note with that title, including a title with spaces. The same text inside a code span, a fenced block, math, or a diagram stays text.</p>

<h3 id="when-will-it-stop">When Will It Stop?</h3>

<p>I&rsquo;m having fun revitalizing this app. I&rsquo;ll stop when nvUltra is ready. Until then it&rsquo;s back to being one of my playgrounds. Hopefully I won&rsquo;t do anything too obtrusive to people who don&rsquo;t need all these extra features. I&rsquo;ll try to contain myself. In the meantime, if you have any requests, the lines are open to callers, either <a href="https://github.com/ttscoff/nv">on GitHub</a> or just <a href="https://brettterpstra.com/contact/">contact me</a>.</p>

